A||||12||ThatRickGuy||cam for 3.2||||5||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||08-21-2000||12:22 PM||||Hey guys, my torn up 2.8 is in the shop right now getting cleaned up, bored over and the 3.1 crank. I know the fiero heads are the HO 2.8 heads with better flow and bigger valves, but will the stock cam be able to feed the new displacement? I'm going for more torque then HP, lets face it, going 140 mph is real cool, but how often can you do it? as compaired to how many stop lights are in DC, and how many civics are parked at all of them? I was planning on porting the exhaust manifolds, at least enough to get rid of the smaller circle where the pipes meet. There's not much I can do about the intake, at the moment, but other then that, is there anything I can/should do? Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||08-21-2000||06:40 PM||||You are better off getting the GM Performance cam 12363220 used in the 3.4 crate motor or the Crane 2030. The stock cam will work fine but it will fall flat on its face after 5000 RPM. You will get gobs of low-end torque though and great gas mileage. <P>I ran the numbers on my dyno simulation program for you and I got 144 HP @ 4500 RPM and 206 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 RPM for the stock 2.8 camshaft. The GM 12363220 camshaft made 160 HP @ 5000 RPM and 196-FT. lbs. of torque @ 3500 RPM. The Crane 2030 made 160 HP @ 5000 RPM and 198 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 RPM. <P>If you are looking for something that will give you a lot of low-end power with decent upper end power you may want to look at the Crane 2020 also. The crane 2020 made 154 HP @ 5000 RPM and 202 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 RPM.<P>Hope this helps you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||08-21-2000||08:35 PM||||can you simulate which one would win in a drag race, 1/8 mile and 1/4.... I would think that the stock cam would win off the line but the performance would win after a short distance.... what are your opinions on this?||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-21-2000||10:15 PM||||--------Quarter mile time and MPH--------0-60<BR>Stock cam-----------15.3 @ 85 MPH--------7.3<BR>GM 12363220---------14.7 @ 91 MPH--------6.5<BR>Crane 2030----------14.7 @ 90 MPH--------6.5<BR>Crane 2020----------14.8 @ 89 MPH--------6.7<BR>Stock 2.8-----------15.7 @ 84 MPH--------7.9<BR>2.8 with 50 nitrous-14.1 @ 95 MPH--------5.6<BR>Stock 3.4-----------14.6 @ 91 MPH--------6.3<BR>3.4 with 50 nitrous-13.4 @ 101 MPH-------5.0<P>This is with an 8.8 compression ratio, stock heads, 3.530 bore and 3.310 stroke, and high performance manifolds and mufflers. The model I used had a five-speed transmission (typed in the correct ratios) with the 3.61 axle ratio and a curb weight of 2925 lbs., which includes the driver. This program also assumes that the air-fuel ratios are perfect.<P>Yes the stock cam would barely beat the 2030 cam off the line.<P>The 3.4 I am building should do the quarter mile in 14.1 seconds @ 95 MPH and 0-60 in 5.8 seconds.<BR>   <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||08-21-2000||11:11 PM||||I'm doing the same motor you're doing... I've already decided on the GM cam as well.  So far it's the best that I can find for this application.||||reg||1
