Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||08-22-2000||10:48 AM||||Thanks for the help guys. I think I'll stay stock for right now. Like I said, I'm not huge on high speed racing, 75 on interstate is good enough for me, off the line is what I was looking for. I like high speed and all, but after the fun of my '76 lemans (torque monster) I would really miss that low end, sucked into the seat take offs. 200 ft/lbs is a bit less then what the Lemans was running, but then again, the lemans was just short of 2000lbs heavy w/ me in it. ||||reg||10
Z||000007||88formula||08-22-2000||05:32 PM||||HYPTNOTiSE, you have to give me more information than that. I did the best I could to fill in the blanks and here is what I got. <P>This is with an 8.8 compression ratio, stock heads, 3.530 bore and 3.310 stroke, and high performance manifolds and mufflers. The cam Specs I used are 204/216 duration and .454"/. 480" lift with a 112-degree lobe center. I filled in the intake centerline as 105 degrees.<P>160 HP from 4500-5000 RPM and 204 ft. lbs. of torque from 3000-3500 RPM <P>It will run the quarter mile in 14.8 seconds @ 89.7 MPH and accelerate to 60 in 6.6 seconds. This cam makes real strong low-end torque and takes off the line quickly. It reaches 60 feet in 2.1 seconds.<P>GT bastard, what are you doing about your fuel system as far as injectors and prom chips?<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||HYPTNOTiSE||08-22-2000||09:04 PM||||88 Formula this is what i have... Sorry.<P>NOW it's a 3.1 liter .30 over, O ringed block, Forged Venoila Pistons, dry film coated skirts,Total Seal nitrous rings, Polished Small Block Chevy Rods, Stock FWD 3.1 Crank(tuftrided, radiused, balanced), Splayed Main Caps on #'s 2&3,A.R.P. Main Studs, Crane Cam with .454/.480 lift with a split duration of 204/216 and 112 degree lobe seperation, Cloyes Double Roller Timing Chain, HEADS: pocket ported, polished, bowl blended,  reprofiled stainless steel non magnetic racing valves, Hardened Bronze Guides, A.R.P. Head Stud kit, radius valve job, 1.6 Roller Rockers, chromemoly pushrods, Hi-Po Valve Springs and Retainers, Ported intake manifolds, Ported 57mm Darrell Morse Throttlebody, with a Custom Superchips PROM, 30# injectors, Adjustable Fuel Pressure Regulator, Split Arc-2 Air/Fuel calibation piggyback control module, Aluminum Cradle Bushings, Poly Motor and solid Tranny Mounts, Clutchnet..kevlar fiber clutch with 2100 pounds of pressure on the plate, 3.83 Final Drive in Isuzu Trans 1-5th gear ratios listed respectivly: 3.73,2.04,1.45,1.03,.74 again with a 3.83 final drive ratio instad of 3.35),Jacobs Electronics Pro-Street Ignition System, Custom Billet Underdrive Pulleys, Modified Borla Exhaust, Custom Downpipe to the Borla exhuast system, Custom Headers and Cross-over pipe made my Steve Hamm, K&N filter, with and without 100hp nitrous system.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||HYPTNOTiSE||08-22-2000||09:07 PM||||oh whoops and 14:1 compression. j/k!<P>my compression ratio is 9.6:1<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
Z||000010||ThatRickGuy||08-23-2000||04:18 PM||||Okay, any of you guys with Desktop Dyno, Heres what the shop recomended for the cam, anyone nice enough to throw this on real quick?<P>The engine is a 2.8, bored .030 w/ the 3.1 crank, slightly ported exhaust manifolds, and no cat. the cam is: <BR>Valve Lift: 420in/443out<BR>Duration (Advertised): 278/288<BR>Duration @50: 204/214<BR>Lobe Sep: 107/117<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000011||88formula||08-23-2000||06:44 PM||||Both of you guys I need the intake centerline to give you more accurate results so I filled it in as 105 degrees.<P>HYPTNOTiSE, I got 199 HP @ 5000 RPM and 230 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4000 RPM. I used the ported head flow numbers from Norris Racing web-site. You are going all out with this rebuild, man!<P>If you launch @ 4500 RPM, shift 1-2 @ 5500, shift 2-3 @ 6000, shift 3-4 @ 6000, and 4-5 @ 5500 you should get a 13.661 @ 98.2 mph and 0-60 in 5.2 seconds.  Launching @ 4500 rpm and shifting the rest @ 6000 rpm with a Muncie 282 5-speed you will get 13.678 @ 97.4 mph and 0-60 in 5.32 seconds.<P>I am assuming that the .454/.480 lift is with 1.6 rocker arms.<P>Can you give me those part numbers for the ARP main bolts and rod bolts?<P>ThatRickGuy, I got 164 HP @ 5000 rpm and 199 ft. lbs. Of torque @ 3500 rpm. I used the stock head flow numbers from Norris Racing web-site and high performance manifolds and mufflers. With the Muncie 282 5-speed you will get 14.5 @ 91.1 mph and 0-60 in 6.3 seconds.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||HYPTNOTiSE||08-23-2000||07:49 PM||||I dont have the part number for the rod bolts or the head/main studs. But i can tell anyone who listens this.  If you want quality main studs, buy a head stud kit for arp, you'll have a few left over short studs and a few nuts, but The longer head studs work great as main studs.<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
